1. real mode:
    how it start:
    the first 512 bytes from the hard drive,
    those bytes the legacy bios he into address memory 
    0x7c00:
        first byte:
            low nibble: 0111 >> in hex 7
            high nibble: 1101 >> in hex 'c'
        secend byte:
            low nibble: 0000 >> in hex 0
            high nibble: 0000 >> in hex 0
    and jump to there (0x7c00)
    the cpu behaving now like he is a old cpu 8086,
    you have only access to 1 MB of the memory

    what can i do:
        here you still allowed to use on "interrputs" of the bios like:
        int 0x10
        to print text to the display

    What the code needs to do at this point:
        Reset the Segment Registers (ds, es, ss) to 0.
        Set up the Stack so you can use call and ret.
        Read the rest of the bootloader/kernel code from disk (because 512 bytes is not enough
         for the 64-bit transition). 
        Enable the A20 line (a historical hack that allows access to memory beyond 1MB).